 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d056: knot (k=10)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.6 and radius 2.00 with 10 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.39) and a 4-dim charge; 12 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 32 couplings (38% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.90 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 1.00 bonds per particle, largest molecule 5.5 particles, 13.0 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.110 vs ESN 1.000\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.110  random bonds 0.108\n  xor      0.554 vs ESN 0.523\u00b10.006  [beat]  no bonds 0.509  random bonds 0.509\n  narma10  0.326 vs ESN 0.845\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.337  random bonds 0.326\n  latch    0.929 vs ESN 0.684\u00b10.008  [beat]  no bonds 0.924  random bonds 0.929\n  mnist14  0.579 vs ESN 0.856\u00b10.010  [below]  no bonds 0.551  random bonds 0.582\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly matters on xor: the bonds its copies chose beat both no bonds and random bonds.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
